.TH test 1 (date) "GNU coreutils (unreleased)"
.SH NAME
test \- check file types and compare values
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY test
.I EXPRESSION
.YS
.SY test
.YS
.\" \& tells doclifter the brackets are literal (Bug#31803).
.SY [\&
.I EXPRESSION
.B ]\&
.YS
.SY [\&
.B ]\&
.YS
.SY [\&
.I OPTION
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.\" Add any additional description here
.PP
Exit with the status determined by EXPRESSION.
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/test#test\-\-help
\fB\-\-help\fP
.UE
display this help and exit
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/test#test\-\-version
\fB\-\-version\fP
.UE
output version information and exit
.PP
An omitted EXPRESSION defaults to false.  Otherwise,
EXPRESSION is true or false and sets exit status.  It is one of:
.TP
( EXPRESSION )
EXPRESSION is true
.TP
! EXPRESSION
EXPRESSION is false
.TP
EXPRESSION1 \fB\-a\fR EXPRESSION2
both EXPRESSION1 and EXPRESSION2 are true
.TP
EXPRESSION1 \fB\-o\fR EXPRESSION2
either EXPRESSION1 or EXPRESSION2 is true
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-n
\fB\-n STRING\fP
.UE
the length of STRING is nonzero
.TP
STRING
equivalent to \fB\-n\fR STRING
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-z
\fB\-z STRING\fP
.UE
the length of STRING is zero
.TP
STRING1 = STRING2
the strings are equal
.TP
STRING1 != STRING2
the strings are not equal
.TP
STRING1 > STRING2
STRING1 is greater than STRING2 in the current locale
.TP
STRING1 < STRING2
STRING1 is less than STRING2 in the current locale
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-eq
\fBINTEGER1 \-eq INTEGER2\fP
.UE
INTEGER1 is equal to INTEGER2
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-ge
\fBINTEGER1 \-ge INTEGER2\fP
.UE
INTEGER1 is greater than or equal to INTEGER2
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-gt
\fBINTEGER1 \-gt INTEGER2\fP
.UE
INTEGER1 is greater than INTEGER2
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-le
\fBINTEGER1 \-le INTEGER2\fP
.UE
INTEGER1 is less than or equal to INTEGER2
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-lt
\fBINTEGER1 \-lt INTEGER2\fP
.UE
INTEGER1 is less than INTEGER2
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-ne
\fBINTEGER1 \-ne INTEGER2\fP
.UE
INTEGER1 is not equal to INTEGER2
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-ef
\fBFILE1 \-ef FILE2\fP
.UE
FILE1 and FILE2 have the same device and inode numbers
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-nt
\fBFILE1 \-nt FILE2\fP
.UE
FILE1 is newer (modification date) than FILE2
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-ot
\fBFILE1 \-ot FILE2\fP
.UE
FILE1 is older than FILE2
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-b
\fB\-b FILE\fP
.UE
FILE exists and is block special
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-c
\fB\-c FILE\fP
.UE
FILE exists and is character special
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-d
\fB\-d FILE\fP
.UE
FILE exists and is a directory
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-e
\fB\-e FILE\fP
.UE
FILE exists
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-f
\fB\-f FILE\fP
.UE
FILE exists and is a regular file
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-g
\fB\-g FILE\fP
.UE
FILE exists and is set\-group\-ID
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-G
\fB\-G FILE\fP
.UE
FILE exists and is owned by the effective group ID
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-h
\fB\-h FILE\fP
.UE
FILE exists and is a symbolic link (same as \fB\-L\fR)
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-k
\fB\-k FILE\fP
.UE
FILE exists and has its sticky bit set
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-L
\fB\-L FILE\fP
.UE
FILE exists and is a symbolic link (same as \fB\-h\fR)
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-N
\fB\-N FILE\fP
.UE
FILE exists and has been modified since it was last read
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-O
\fB\-O FILE\fP
.UE
FILE exists and is owned by the effective user ID
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-p
\fB\-p FILE\fP
.UE
FILE exists and is a named pipe
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-r
\fB\-r FILE\fP
.UE
FILE exists and the user has read access
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-s
\fB\-s FILE\fP
.UE
FILE exists and has a size greater than zero
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-S
\fB\-S FILE\fP
.UE
FILE exists and is a socket
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-t
\fB\-t FD\fP
.UE
file descriptor FD is opened on a terminal
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-u
\fB\-u FILE\fP
.UE
FILE exists and its set\-user\-ID bit is set
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-w
\fB\-w FILE\fP
.UE
FILE exists and the user has write access
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#test\-x
\fB\-x FILE\fP
.UE
FILE exists and the user has execute (or search) access
.PP
Except for \fB\-h\fR and \fB\-L\fR, all FILE\-related tests dereference symbolic links.
Beware that parentheses need to be escaped (e.g., by backslashes) for shells.
INTEGER may also be \fB\-l\fR STRING, which evaluates to the length of STRING.
.PP
Binary \fB\-a\fR and \fB\-o\fR are ambiguous.  Use 'test EXPR1 && test EXPR2'
or 'test EXPR1 || test EXPR2' instead.
.PP
\&'[' honors \fB\-\-help\fR and \fB\-\-version\fR, but 'test' treats them as STRINGs.
.PP
Your shell may have its own version of test and/or [, which usually supersedes
the version described here.  Please refer to your shell's documentation
for details about the options it supports.
